steps.body:
REDUCTIONISM is the approach where you break a system into parts and then study how those parts interact, in order to understand the whole. Its core claim: «phenomena can be exhaustively explained in terms of other, more fundamental phenomena».

It has been extraordinarily successful. You can take a plane apart down to the last screw and understand how it flies. You can understand a program by analysing its code. Scientists study diseases and birth defects by analysing the human genome, hoping to identify the individual genes responsible for particular problems. Almost everything technical you know was built this way.

But the approach works only up to a certain limit, and the book lists where it stops:
• after many years of research, scientists still do not understand how consciousness works;
• despite a hundred years of theories, economists could not build a model that reliably predicts crises;
• numerous climate models produce completely different forecasts of the consequences of global warming;
• and although there is no shortage of methods modelling software development, projects all over the world still run into unforeseen problems.

Living organisms, human consciousness, the economy, climate change and software projects have one thing in common: their behaviour cannot be predicted by deconstructing them and studying the components separately.

Think about a place where a «low-level» problem leaked into a «high-level» one:

• a small technical bug that became a client scandal;
• one person's bad mood that changed a whole team meeting;
• a tiny process detail (an unclear form, a missing access right) that broke a big plan.

Write one such story in three lines: what was the low-level cause, how it leaked upwards, and what would have made the higher level more robust.

The purpose is to train the habit of asking: «where can our nice clean abstraction leak?»

Imagine a friend from another profession asks: «What is this Agile everybody talks about?»

Explain in one minute, in simple words, using:
• the problem it answers (heavy formal methods, slow feedback, changing requirements);
• the four values of the manifesto in your own words;
• the important nuance: it is not «no rules» — it is a middle way between bureaucracy and chaos.

If your explanation sounds like «you just do whatever you want, quickly» — go back to the manifesto step and try again.

Quality plays a decisive role in the success of a product, so at the centre of attention in agile methods stands TECHNICAL EXCELLENCE. A high technical level is achieved through a set of practices:

• development through testing — the test protocol of the finished product is written BEFORE the software code itself;
• code review, often in combination with pair programming;
• Definition of Done — a checklist of readiness for items;
• iterative development — adapting code as a result of changes or other circumstances;
• refactoring — continuous optimisation of code even when there are no changes in functionality.

Supporters of agile methods also recognise the necessity of sequential design improvement: at the start of a project the architecture of the product is not developed in detail (only in a basic form) and is revealed during the further development of the project.

This is the dimension most often forgotten by managers who like the word «agile» for its speed. The plan is flexible; the quality discipline is not. Without these practices, short cycles simply produce broken software faster.

Although the dominant paradigm of agile methods is people rather than processes, this does not at all mean that processes are unimportant.

The most important processes in this context are:
• minimal planning, or planning by the «rolling wave» method — detail near work, direction for far work;
• daily personal communication, often in the format of daily stand-ups;
• monitoring the progress of the project through the evaluation of WORKING software — by functional capabilities accepted by the client;
• continuous improvement, in the course of which development processes are regularly re-evaluated and reconfigured through analysis and retrospectives.

Notice the third point especially. Progress is measured by working functionality accepted by the client — not by tasks closed, hours spent or percentages reported. This single rule removes most of the ways a project can look healthy while being badly late.

After this course you can:

• see organisations as networks, not only as org charts;
• recognise Management 1.0 and 2.0 patterns around you — and name them;
• notice linear thinking and the causality fallacy in yourself and others;
• tell the difference between what can be calculated and what can only be tried;
• use the six views (energise, empower, align, develop, grow, improve) as a practical checklist;
• explain what Agile really is, in seven dimensions — including quality and discipline;
• choose between agile, lean and craftsmanship ideas depending on context.

That is a lot for a book that promised «no simple recipes».
